{"name":"Cranberry Sauce","hex":"#a53756","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cranberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub belonging to the section Vaccinium sect. Oxycoccus of the genus Vaccinium.","The edible red berry of that shrub."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the intense red colour of a cranberry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cranberry"},{"term":"sauce","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A liquid (often thickened) condiment or accompaniment to food.","Tomato sauce (similar to US tomato ketchup), as in:"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add sauce to; to season.","To cause to relish anything, as if with a sauce; to tickle or gratify, as the palate; to please; to stimulate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sauce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cranberry sauce","blocks":[{"text":"Cranberry sauce or cranberry jam is a sauce or relish made out of cranberries, commonly served as a condiment or a side dish with Thanksgiving dinner in North America and Christmas dinner in the United Kingdom and Canada. There may be subtle differences in flavor depending on the geography of where the sauce is made: in Europe it is generally slightly sour-tasting, while in North America it is typically more heavily sweetened."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Cranberry_Sauce_%283617909597%29.jpg/330px-Cranberry_Sauce_%283617909597%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranberry_sauce"},{"title":"Cranberry","blocks":[{"text":"Cranberries are a group of evergreen dwarf shrubs or trailing vines in the subgenus Oxycoccus of the genus Vaccinium. Cranberries are low, creeping shrubs or vines up to 2 meters (7 ft) long and 5 to 20 centimeters in height; they have slender stems that are not thickly woody and have small evergreen leaves. The flowers are dark pink. The fruit is a berry that is larger than the leaves of the plant; it is initially light green, turning red when ripe. The raw berry is edible and has a tart taste due to the low sugar content and high acidity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Cranberry_bog.jpg/330px-Cranberry_bog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranberry"},{"title":"Sauce","blocks":[{"text":"In cooking, a sauce is a liquid, cream, or semi-solid food, served on or used in preparing other foods. Most sauces are not normally consumed by themselves; they add flavour, texture, and visual appeal to a dish. Sauce is a French word probably from the post-classical Latin salsa, derived from the classical salsus 'salted'. Possibly the oldest recorded European sauce is garum, the fish sauce used by the Ancient Romans, while doubanjiang, the Chinese soy bean paste, is mentioned in Rites of Zhou 20."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lunch_sauces.jpg/330px-Lunch_sauces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauce"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Making_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg/330px-Making_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Making_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg/960px-Making_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Making_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg/330px-Making_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaking_cranberry_sauce_-_stovetop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1